  "text": "Add New Proposal Shows Add New Show View All Shows 17 May 2026 - Carte Blanche HD 10 May 2026 - Carte Blanche HD 03 May 2026 - Carte Blanche HD 26 April 2026 - Carte Blanche HD 19 April 2026 - Carte Blanche HD 12 April 2026 - Carte Blanche HD 05 April 2026 - Carte Blanche HD Hi Lezanne [My Account] [Logout] Home Viewer Submissions Reports Contacts Music Cue Sheet Manage Users View Insert Edit Insert Insert Workflow Anti microbial resistance: Threat to humanity. By: Mart-Marie Faure | Commission Proposal Show: Carte Blanche One Liner (Tonight on Carte Blanche...): Will so called super bugs become the biggest threat to humanity in the twenty first century? Unique Selling Point: The threat of so-called super bugs is a major challenge facing humanity in the twenty-first century. That is according to the World Health Organisation. The misuse and overuse of antibiotics in medicine and food production has created antimicrobial resistance making some diseases almost untreatable leading to millions of deaths in the future. Summary: Superbugs are strains of bacteria, viruses, parasites and fungi that are resistant to most of the antibiotics and other medications commonly used to treat the infections they cause. A few examples of superbugs include resistant bacteria that can cause pneumonia, urinary tract infections and skin infections. Research has show that to date, the environment has been the neglected component in the one-health triad, One health triad being human, animal and environment. The transmission of AMR is probably multi-directional, Research is currently being done on wastewater as well as soil that’s been fertilised with the manure from pigs and poultry fed with antimicrobials as well as environmental surveillance within wastewater close to informal settlements and hospital effluent and wastewater treatment plants, focusing on the environmental dimensions. Extra Budget Items: Flights to Durban 1 Nights accommodation in Durban Car Hire Case Study: Ing